                  "system": "http://loinc.org",
                  "display": "Summarization of encounter note"
                }
              ]
            },
            "title": "Synthesis"
          },
          {
            "text": {
              "status": "generated",
              "div": "<div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\">Dear colleague, I am writing to inform you about the hospital course of your 75-year-old female patient, admitted on 21/02/1992 with acute lower abdominal pain and haemodynamic instability. Clinical examination and transvaginal ultrasonography confirmed a tubal ectopic pregnancy with suspected rupture. Laboratory investigations including serum beta-hCG and blood tests supported the diagnosis. Given her clinical deterioration, the patient was taken to the operating theatre on 21/02/1992, where laparoscopic salpingectomy for tubal ectopic pregnancy was successfully performed. The postoperative course was uneventful. Vital signs remained stable throughout recovery. Wound care was provided, and the patient was mobilised appropriately. She was discharged home on 23/02/1992 in good general condition with outpatient follow-up arranged. Please do not hesitate to contact me should you require further information.</div>"
            },
